A||N||6||fsrowsell||flushing the brake system||||1||
Z||000000||fsrowsell||03-22-2001||07:42 PM||||Anyone have any good suggestions on how to flush the brake system in an 86SE. Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||03-22-2001||08:35 PM||||I use a hand-operated vacuum pump (the kind designed for brake bleeding and lots of other stuff).  Just connect it to the bleeder, pump up some vacuum, then crack the bleeder open.  Just make sure you check and top up the master cylinder as necesary.  It usually takes about a quart of DOT 3.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-22-2001||10:48 PM||||Use the newer high temp DOT 3 formulas. you could also consider valvoline synthetic. Either will significantly raise the boiling point of the fluid above the old DOT 3 spec.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fsrowsell||03-22-2001||11:46 PM||||Mark, is it best that I do each wheel until the brake fluid is clear? Then move on to the next wheel, until I have all four wheels done. What do you think?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||03-23-2001||07:51 AM||||If the fluid in the master cylinder is very dirty I'd suck about half of it out using a Turkey Baster and refill with fresh fluid.  Do this a few times to clean out the master cylinder reservoir prior to flushing the lines.  You might even use a clean rag and wipe the black gook from the walls of the master cylinder tank while trying not to let any fall in.  Then flush each line until clear fluid arrives.  Start with the farthest from the master cylinder first (rr wheel) then move on to the next farthest and so on.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark||03-23-2001||08:39 AM||||Ditto what Carrolles said about first sucking the dirty brake fluid out of the master cyl. Good point! I use the same vacuum pump tool for this (this tool really is handy).  Just make sure the hose or whatever you stick down into the reservoir is clean. I generally pull about a 8 ounces of fluid through each bleeder, maybe a little more through the first (right rear) and a little less through the last (left front).<P>Ogre, I was not aware there is a "better" DOT 3 out there.  I'll have to look into this next time.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-23-2001||07:35 PM||||I suck out the MC tank until it's down to the opening into the MC itself. Then I carefully wipe the thing out with a soft rag, preferably terry cloth, that will trap the dirt. wipe away from the MC opening. This make short work of it. <P>Don't try to drain out all the fluid in the tank. you don't want to accidently let air into the MC.<P>Then you bleed each line until new fluid flows.<P>There are several newer formulas compatible with DOT 3. All of them will increase boiling temp over regular DOT 3.||||reg||1
